  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 227 AM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows high pressure in place \nacross NY and PA. Low pressure was found over western Ontario, with \na cold front that extended south into the upper Mississippi Valley. \nGOES19 shows high cloud across central Indiana and Illinois. Within \nthe upper flow strong ridging remained in place over the Rockies \nwith NW flow spilling across the northern plains into the Ohio \nValley. Within this flow water vapor shows an upper low just north \nof MN with a trough axis across MN. This was causing a few showers \nand storms across central WI.\n\nToday and this evening...\n\nMuch of the day will remain dry today as Indiana remains under the \ninfluence of the surface high pressure to the east. The cold front \nto the north will make steady progress toward Central Indiana today, \nbut should not impact our area until this evening. HRRR suggests \nshower and storm development near 22Z, late in the day. Forecast \nsoundings show best conditions for convection shortly after diurnal \nmaximum, with CAPE over 1800 J/KG. Thus will expect partly cloudy \nskies through the day along with dry weather, but will add pops \nafter 22Z through the evening hours as the thin line of convection \npushes across the area. Confidence for rain is medium, with best \nchances across the eastern half of the forecast area.\n\nOvernight through Friday...\n\nIn the wake of the front dry air, subsidence and Canadian high \npressure will build across Indiana through Friday. This will result \nin more dry weather with mostly sunny days and mostly clear nights. \nMainly north to northeasterly surface flow will be in place and this \nwill keep our high temperatures mainly in the lower 80s, providing \ncomfortable conditions.\n\nSaturday through Sunday...\n\nModels suggest that on Saturday and Sunday surface high pressure \nsets up off the east coast of the CONUS, allowing the development of \nwarmer and more humid air arriving across Central Indiana on \nsoutherly winds. Mid levels suggest good subsidence still remains in \nplace across Indiana. Strong ridging within the upper flow will be \nin place over MN and central Canada, resulting in lee side \nsubsidence spilling into Indiana and the southern Great Lakes. Thus \ndry weather is expected on both Saturday and Sunday given this \npattern. Expect above normal highs with temperatures in the upper \n80s approaching 90.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...\n\nBy Monday and Tuesday the upper pattern appears to flatten, \nstreaming from the high plains to the Great Lakes. This results in a \nloss of the mid level subsidence and Pacific moisture looks to begin \nto stream into the region. A frontal boundary is suggested to set up \nNW of Indiana stretching from MI to MO, placing Indiana in the warm \nsector with continued warm and humid flow. Early forecast soundings \nsuggest steep lapse rates with CAPE available. Thus given this, \ndiurnal convection cannot be ruled out and small pops will likely be \nneeded. Highs around 90 will be possible.",
